WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.051
Episode two: your first case.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:06.153
This video assumes APEX installed as the README describes.

3
00:00:06.153 --> 00:00:09.336
Run every command below from the APEX folder.

4
00:00:09.336 --> 00:00:13.451
That is episode one.

5
00:00:13.451 --> 00:00:17.314
This video and the next three follow one page of the documentation.

6
00:00:17.314 --> 00:00:27.970
By the end of this page you have run a complete history match in a terminal, from a copy of an example to its summary, report and figures.

7
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:37.343
The case is APEX's linear-Gaussian example: a linear model y = G x with 3 unknowns and 8 data, a Gaussian prior and Gaussian data errors.

8
00:00:37.343 --> 00:00:40.900
It needs no reservoir simulator and runs in seconds.

9
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:49.826
Because the model is linear and Gaussian, its exact answer (the Kalman posterior) is known.

10
00:00:49.826 --> 00:00:53.229
Work on a copy, so that the example stays as it is.

11
00:00:53.229 --> 00:01:03.810
We copy it to a folder called my_first_case.

12
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.003
This removes runs you made in the example, e.g.

13
00:01:07.003 --> 00:01:16.378
by the README's step 6, if there are any.

14
00:01:16.378 --> 00:01:29.693
make_case.py writes the case's files next to itself: the forward model G (forward.npz), the observed data with their error variances (observations.csv) and the true model that generated them (truth.npz).

15
00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:43.935
It also prints the exact answer to compare with later.

16
00:01:43.935 --> 00:01:56.805
Here are the observed data with their error variances: one row per datum.

17
00:01:56.805 --> 00:02:12.659
my_first_case is a folder of your own: git status lists it as untracked, and an APEX update leaves it alone.

18
00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:14.141
Now the configuration.

19
00:02:14.141 --> 00:02:16.566
A run is described by one TOML file.

20
00:02:16.566 --> 00:02:22.090
[run]: the run's name (linear_esmda), which names its folder, and the random seed.

21
00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:31.482
The same seed gives the same results.

22
00:02:31.482 --> 00:02:42.889
[[state.variables]]: the unknowns, here one variable x of size 3.

23
00:02:42.889 --> 00:02:55.004
[prior]: the ensemble, 500 members drawn from a standard normal distribution.

24
00:02:55.004 --> 00:03:05.681
[observations]: the data, from observations.csv.

25
00:03:05.681 --> 00:03:20.953
[case]: a synthetic case, with the true model in truth.npz, so that runs also report how far the members are from the truth (RMSE).

26
00:03:20.953 --> 00:03:35.301
[simulator]: the model that turns each member into predicted data, here the linear model in forward.npz.

27
00:03:35.301 --> 00:03:46.917
[execution]: where the members run, here 4 processes on this machine.

28
00:03:46.917 --> 00:04:06.951
[method]: the history-matching method, ES-MDA in 4 steps (inflation = [4, 4, 4, 4]), with its update strategy.

29
00:04:06.951 --> 00:04:11.529
So: a copy of your own, its files, and one file that describes the run.

30
00:04:11.529 --> 00:04:18.742
In the next video we check what the run will cost, and run part of it.

31
00:04:18.742 --> 00:04:24.242
Thank you for watching.
